; SOL — the development and calibration system.
;
; There is no Sol in the shipped game: every system the player visits is generated. This file
; exists so the derivation can be built and falsified against real values (Earth's 288 K, Venus's
; 737 K, the Moon's day/night contrast) before a generator exists.
;
; SOURCES. Physical data (mass, radius, rotation, obliquity, surface pressure, composition) from
; the NASA/NSSDC planetary fact sheets, 2025-03-18 edition. Orbital elements at J2000 from JPL
; SSD "Approximate Positions of the Planets" (Table 1, mean ecliptic and equinox of J2000, valid
; 1800-2050), converted here to argument of periapsis (longitude of perihelion minus longitude of
; ascending node) and mean anomaly at epoch (mean longitude minus longitude of perihelion).
;
; CONVENTIONS. Metres, kilograms, seconds, kelvin, pascals, degrees. A layer's `radius` is its
; OUTER BOUNDARY. `rotation_period` is always positive: retrograde is `axial_tilt > 90` (Venus
; 177.4, Uranus 97.77). Angles are degrees.
;
; THREE THINGS THAT ARE DELIBERATELY INCOMPLETE:
;
; 1. MASS IS AUTHORED FOR EVERY BODY. The derivation should produce mass from the layers, and cannot
;    yet: a constant-density layer model ignores self-compression, so Earth comes out at 4.449e24 kg
;    against a real 5.972e24 — 26% light, because the real inner core is ~13,000 kg/m3 rather than
;    iron's 7,874 at room pressure. That error would put every moon's period 12% out, so the real
;    masses are authored and the layers stand ready for when a compression model exists. Gas giants
;    have a second reason: a smoothly compressed hydrogen envelope is not a constant-density layer
;    either. 0.0 still means "derive it", and nothing does yet.
; 2. MOON ORBITS ARE EXPRESSED IN THE SYSTEM FRAME, not around their planet's equator. A moon's
;    natural reference plane is its planet's equatorial or Laplace plane, and using it needs the
;    planet's pole ORIENTATION — the content carries an obliquity but not the direction of the pole.
;    Until it does, published inclinations are used as-is against the system frame, which for the
;    Galileans is worth about three degrees (Jupiter's own obliquity). Each moon's initial mean
;    anomaly is not sourced and is 0: that places it somewhere on its orbit rather than nowhere.
;    Their interior structures are also much less well constrained than the planets', and are
;    approximate.
; 3. THE SUN'S LAYERS ARE ONE BULK LAYER. Its mass is authored, so no density profile is needed to
;    get mass; the layer exists to carry composition, which the spectral and temperature derivation
;    reads. A core/radiative/convective split adds nothing the model currently uses.
;
; The moons' periods are a real check rather than decoration: from a real semi-major axis and a real
; parent mass they come out within 0.2% of the measured values (Luna 27.28 days, Io 1.769,
; Ganymede 7.155, Titan 15.951).
;
; Trace solar abundances are approximate: hydrogen and helium are the standard mass fractions, the
; minor species are close but not sourced to the last digit.

[sub_resource type="Resource" id="Framing_sol"]
script = ExtResource("framing")
system_band = Vector2(10000, 10000000000000)
atmosphere_band = Vector2(2000, 400000)
surface_band = Vector2(100, 5000)
atmosphere_altitude = 100000.0
surface_altitude = 2000.0

