; ---------- atmospheres ----------

[sub_resource type="Resource" id="A_venus"]
; **Venus is why `atmospheric_rotation_period` has to exist.** Its air superrotates -- it circles the
; planet in about 4 days while the planet itself takes 243, a factor of 61 -- which moves its band scale
; from ~31,800 km to ~4,080 and its cells from 0.07 to 0.58. `wind_speed` is the ~100 m/s
; superrotation, which is what puts its band scale at the planet's own size and makes the model say
; FEATURELESS. **Both rotations give FEATURELESS**, so the field is about the numbers a map is built
; from rather than about the verdict; `tests/test_clouds.gd` asserts both. See `SpacesimCloudModel`.
script = ExtResource("atmosphere")
surface_pressure = 9200000.0
species = Dictionary[String, float]({
"carbon_dioxide": 0.965,
"nitrogen": 0.035
})
atmospheric_rotation_period = 345600.0
wind_speed = 100.0

[sub_resource type="Resource" id="A_earth"]
; **Ozone's mole fraction is DERIVED from the column, not chosen.** The model treats every absorber as
; well mixed with the air, while the real ozone sits in a layer around 25 km -- so the honest way to put
; the right amount in is to match the COLUMN: Earth's 300 DU is 8.06e22 molecules per square metre, and a
; well-mixed profile needs `n_surface * H = 8.06e22`, which at 288 K and one atmosphere is 3.8e-7.
script = ExtResource("atmosphere")
surface_pressure = 101325.0
species = Dictionary[String, float]({
"argon": 0.00934,
"carbon_dioxide": 0.00036,
"nitrogen": 0.78084,
"oxygen": 0.20946,
"ozone": 0.00000038,
"water_vapour": 0.01
})
variable_species = PackedStringArray("water_vapour")
; A synoptic eddy velocity, ~10 m/s: the velocity scale the band law takes, not a peak wind. Earth has
; no `atmospheric_rotation_period`, so its air turns with the body and `rotation_period` is right.
wind_speed = 10.0

[sub_resource type="Resource" id="A_mars"]
script = ExtResource("atmosphere")
surface_pressure = 610.0
species = Dictionary[String, float]({
"argon": 0.0193,
"carbon_dioxide": 0.9527,
"nitrogen": 0.028
})
; Mars's mean wind, from the dust-storm climatology. It has no deck to move yet - there is no
; water in its mix - so this is here for the day there is.
wind_speed = 10.0

[sub_resource type="Resource" id="A_titan"]
script = ExtResource("atmosphere")
surface_pressure = 146700.0
species = Dictionary[String, float]({
"hydrogen": 0.0025,
"methane": 0.0555,
"nitrogen": 0.942
})
; Titan's winds are very light: about 1 m/s at the surface, and the superrotating jet aloft is
; a separate thing this model does not carry.
wind_speed = 1.0

[sub_resource type="Resource" id="A_triton"]
script = ExtResource("atmosphere")
surface_pressure = 1.4
species = Dictionary[String, float]({
"methane": 0.0001,
"nitrogen": 0.9999
})
; Triton's thin nitrogen atmosphere; a light wind is all its 1.4 Pa can move.
wind_speed = 10.0

; ---------- gas giants ----------
;
; **A giant's "surface" is the 1-bar level**, and that is a convention rather than a boundary: there
; is no liquid to land on. Hydrogen's critical point is 33.15 K at 13.2 bar, and the 1-bar level of
; every giant here is far above that, so the H2/He envelope is a **supercritical fluid** from the
; cloud tops down to metallic hydrogen -- no sharp gas/liquid interface exists at any depth
; (Wikipedia, Atmosphere of Jupiter). 1 bar is the standard level for a giant's radius, which is
; what `radius` above already is.
;
; The visible clouds condense between roughly 0.5 and 10 bar (water deepest, then ammonium
; hydrosulfide, then ammonia: the main deck on Jupiter sits near 1.5 bar), so the 1-bar floor sits
; just above what you would actually see. Close enough, and it is the convention.
;
; **Composition is the three species that dominate**, and trace absorbers (ammonia, water, hydrogen
; sulfide) are deliberately absent until their Rayleigh cross-sections exist -- around 0.06% of the
; mix, so the scattering is unaffected, but they matter for the greenhouse stage later.
;
; Temperatures are the **measured** 1-bar values and are **pinned** through `overrides`, because a
; giant radiates more than it receives: Jupiter's equilibrium temperature is ~101 K against a
; measured 165 K, and internal heat is most of the budget. `reference_temperature()` reads the pin.

[sub_resource type="Resource" id="A_jupiter"]
; A ~50 m/s mean zonal wind, against peaks nearer 150: the band law wants the mean. No
; `atmospheric_rotation_period`, because a giant's 9.9-hour rotation *is* its atmosphere's.
script = ExtResource("atmosphere")
surface_pressure = 101325.0
; **Ammonia and water are added because without them Jupiter has no deck to draw OR to colour.** 0.26 %
; NH3 and 0.4 % H2O are the Galileo probe's deep values; the hydrogen takes the difference so the mix
; still sums to one. They are what makes the ammonia deck at ~0.6 bar, the ammonium hydrosulphide deck
; below it and the water deck below that — the stack whose colours ARE Jupiter.
species = Dictionary[String, float]({
"ammonia": 0.0026,
"helium": 0.136,
"hydrogen": 0.8544,
"methane": 0.003,
"water_vapour": 0.004
})
wind_speed = 50.0

[sub_resource type="Resource" id="A_saturn"]
script = ExtResource("atmosphere")
surface_pressure = 101325.0
; Ammonia is **depleted** here relative to Jupiter — 0.02 % against 0.26 % — because Saturn's ammonia
; rains out into the deeper cloud, which is a real measured difference and not a typo.
species = Dictionary[String, float]({
"ammonia": 0.0002,
"helium": 0.0335,
"hydrogen": 0.9608,
"methane": 0.0045,
"water_vapour": 0.001
})
; Saturn's mean zonal wind. Peaks near 400-500 in the equatorial jet; the band law wants
; the mean, the same convention Jupiter's 50 m/s uses.
wind_speed = 100.0

[sub_resource type="Resource" id="A_uranus"]
script = ExtResource("atmosphere")
surface_pressure = 101325.0
species = Dictionary[String, float]({
"helium": 0.15,
"hydrogen": 0.83,
"methane": 0.02
})
; Uranus's mean zonal wind. **Its winds are fast - peaks near 200 - and it is still the blandest
; planet measured**, which is internal heat (§ `internal_heat_flux` 0.042) rather than dynamics. So the
; band law will give it structure and the light will have to earn it back.
wind_speed = 100.0

[sub_resource type="Resource" id="A_neptune"]
script = ExtResource("atmosphere")
surface_pressure = 101325.0
species = Dictionary[String, float]({
"helium": 0.19,
"hydrogen": 0.795,
"methane": 0.015
})
; Neptune's mean zonal wind: the fastest in the system, peaks near 400.
wind_speed = 200.0

[sub_resource type="Resource" id="Ovr_jupiter"]
script = ExtResource("overrides")
set = PackedStringArray("mean_surface_temperature")
mean_surface_temperature = 165.0

[sub_resource type="Resource" id="Ovr_saturn"]
script = ExtResource("overrides")
set = PackedStringArray("mean_surface_temperature")
mean_surface_temperature = 135.0

[sub_resource type="Resource" id="Ovr_uranus"]
script = ExtResource("overrides")
set = PackedStringArray("mean_surface_temperature")
mean_surface_temperature = 76.0

[sub_resource type="Resource" id="Ovr_neptune"]
script = ExtResource("overrides")
set = PackedStringArray("mean_surface_temperature")
mean_surface_temperature = 72.0

