; ---------- cloud decks (authored for now; derived from the atmosphere later)
;
; These carry the albedo as well as the look, because **clouds dominate a planet's reflection**:
; without them Earth derives 0.081 against a measured 0.306. They sit on the body rather than in the
; atmosphere because a gas giant's visible surface *is* its cloud deck -- what the body looks like
; rather than a property of its air. **The giants do carry atmospheres**: all four are authored above
; with 101,325 Pa each. An earlier version of this comment said the opposite, and the same false claim
; had spread to five files before it was caught.
;
; A gas giant's fraction is 1.0 and its albedo is its whole Bond albedo — there is nothing else to
; reflect off. The four giant values are the measured ones: Jupiter 0.50, Saturn 0.34, Uranus 0.30,
; Neptune 0.29.

[sub_resource type="Resource" id="C_venus"]
script = ExtResource("clouds")
fraction = 1.0
albedo = 0.76
color = Color(0.98, 0.92, 0.72, 1)
opacity = 1.0

[sub_resource type="Resource" id="C_earth"]
; **0.419 is the NET reflectance of the cloud system, and it was 0.45 under the gross accounting.**
; `bond_albedo` is net as of 2026-10-16 — the clear column is weighted by the cloud-free fraction and the
; air's own reflection is counted — so the deck's number had to give up the light the clear column it
; hides would have reflected. The value is fitted to hold Earth's measured 0.306 exactly: the fit
; `(0.306 - (1-f)*(surface + air)) / f` is printed by `tests/test_surface.gd`, and the CERES-implied
; deck reflectance for f = 0.6 is 0.389, so this is within 8% of the published split rather than
; 1.9x off it. Nothing else moves: with `fraction = 1.0` the clear term carries no weight at all, so
; Venus and all four giants are untouched by the change.
script = ExtResource("clouds")
fraction = 0.6
albedo = 0.419
color = Color(0.95, 0.96, 1, 1)
opacity = 0.9

[sub_resource type="Resource" id="C_mars"]
; Same fit as Earth's: 0.4 -> 0.488, holding Mars's measured 0.250 under the net accounting. It goes UP
; rather than down because Mars's air reflects only 0.006 — a 610 Pa atmosphere — so its deck carries almost
; all of the reflection the clear column does not.
script = ExtResource("clouds")
fraction = 0.35
albedo = 0.488
color = Color(0.85, 0.78, 0.68, 1)
opacity = 0.6

[sub_resource type="Resource" id="C_titan"]
script = ExtResource("clouds")
fraction = 0.9
albedo = 0.22
color = Color(0.9, 0.72, 0.42, 1)
opacity = 0.5

[sub_resource type="Resource" id="CL_jupiter_ammonia"]
; **The deck you can see: ammonia, at 0.90 bar.** Its colour here is AUTHORED and not the species' own,
; because the giants' visible tint is a chromophore — complex organics and phosphorus compounds mixed
; into the cloud — and **the chromophore is not in the species table**. A pale cream stands in for it,
; which is the honest interim: `SpacesimSpecies.color` supplies a species' true scattering colour, and a
; chromophore needs to exist as a material before it can supply this one.
script = ExtResource("cloud_layer")
species = "ammonia"
coverage = 0.6
albedo = 0.678
color = Color(0.93, 0.89, 0.8, 1)
opacity = 1.0
; The ammonia deck is optically thick in the zones and thin in the belts - which is the belt/zone
; difference, and it now falls out of tau through the field's own thickness rather than a coverage switch.
optical_depth = 1.5

[sub_resource type="Resource" id="CL_jupiter_water"]
; **Water at 7.3 bar, and this is the layer that MAKES THE BELTS.** Where the ammonia above is thin the
; eye reaches this deck, and it is warmer and browner — which is the whole reason belts are dark on a gas
; giant, and why no belt is ever painted onto a latitude. Authored colour, same chromophore caveat.
script = ExtResource("cloud_layer")
species = "water_vapour"
coverage = 0.8
albedo = 0.3
color = Color(0.55, 0.38, 0.24, 1)
opacity = 1.0
; Deeper and denser than the ammonia above it.
optical_depth = 3.0

[sub_resource type="Resource" id="C_jupiter"]
; **A stack, highest first.** The flat fields are left at zero because a body with layers ignores them;
; the two reflectances were fitted so the COMPOSITE holds Jupiter's measured 0.503 Bond albedo, which is
; what `bond_albedo` now computes from the stack rather than from one number.
script = ExtResource("clouds")
fraction = 0.0
albedo = 0.0
color = Color(0.85, 0.78, 0.68, 1)
opacity = 1.0
layers = Array[ExtResource("cloud_layer")]([SubResource("CL_jupiter_ammonia"), SubResource("CL_jupiter_water")])

[sub_resource type="Resource" id="CL_saturn_ammonia"]
script = ExtResource("cloud_layer")
species = "ammonia"
coverage = 0.9
albedo = 0.36
color = Color(0.9, 0.85, 0.7, 1)
opacity = 1.0
; Saturn's ammonia is thinner and more diffuse than Jupiter's.
optical_depth = 3.0

[sub_resource type="Resource" id="CL_saturn_water"]
script = ExtResource("cloud_layer")
species = "water_vapour"
coverage = 0.8
albedo = 0.22
color = Color(0.58, 0.47, 0.33, 1)
opacity = 1.0
optical_depth = 5.0

[sub_resource type="Resource" id="C_saturn"]
; **A stack, because a SINGLE layer cannot show a belt.** With one deck the albedo is uniform, so only the
; alpha varies and the belts are a brightness wobble — Saturn's field measures a row-mean span of 67-238
; levels, exactly as banded as Jupiter's, and drew as a flat disc. A belt is a thin bright deck over a
; browner one, so it takes two. Reflectances fitted to the composite: `0.9*0.36 + 0.1*(0.8*0.22 + ...)`
; lands on Saturn's measured 0.342.
; **Coverage below 1 deliberately** on both: at 1.0 the threshold pins to zero and the deck draws its floor.
script = ExtResource("clouds")
fraction = 0.0
albedo = 0.0
color = Color(0.88, 0.82, 0.65, 1)
opacity = 1.0
layers = Array[ExtResource("cloud_layer")]([SubResource("CL_saturn_ammonia"), SubResource("CL_saturn_water")])

[sub_resource type="Resource" id="C_uranus"]
script = ExtResource("clouds")
fraction = 1.0
albedo = 0.3
color = Color(0.65, 0.85, 0.88, 1)
opacity = 1.0

[sub_resource type="Resource" id="C_neptune"]
; Neptune HAS features - dark spots and fast methane streaks - so its coverage sits below 1 and its
; threshold lands inside the distribution. Uranus keeps 1.0 next door, and that asymmetry is the point:
; Uranus is genuinely the bland one.
script = ExtResource("clouds")
fraction = 0.85
albedo = 0.29
color = Color(0.42, 0.55, 0.85, 1)
opacity = 1.0

